"],"out":"16","g":1},{"in":["7","[5]"],"out":"7","g":1},{"in":["7","[5,2,1,3,4,6]"],"out":"20","g":1},{"in":["1000000","[999999,324257,83781,1]"],"out":"2083781","g":1},{"in":["1000000","[561130,630082,592099]"],"out":"1507822","g":1},{"in":["1000000","[688119,999999,308242]"],"out":"2000000","g":1},{"in":["7","[3,2,6]"],"out":"14","g":1},{"in":["605578","[311875,372885,450358,184481,605577,225121]"],"out":"1632253","g":1},{"in":["38667","[1]"],"out":"38667","g":1},{"in":["26","[1,10,25,18,9,5]"],"out":"70","g":1},{"in":["10","[7]"],"out":"10","g":1},{"in":["650785","[600738]"],"out":"650785","g":1},{"in":["18","[9,16,8,13,2,12,3,5,11]"],"out":"59","g":1},{"in":[],"out":"\"#7:1a42352505d367d1\"","perf":{"label":"100 cuts on a stick of 10⁶","gen":"int a0 = 1000000; int[] a1 = BCB.distinct(100, 1, 999999);","floorMs":2000},"refMs":4}],"lesson":"dp-trees-intervals","anchor":"minimum-cost-to-cut-a-stick","statement":"A wooden stick has length `n` and is marked with positions `0` to `n`. You must cut it at every position listed in `cuts`, in any order you choose. Each cut costs the length of the piece being cut at that moment.\n\nReturn the minimum total cost.","constraints":["2 <= n <= 1e6","1 <= cuts.length <= 100","1 <= cuts[i] < n","The cut positions are distinct"],"hints":["The cost of a cut depends on which cuts happened before it. Think about the first cut made in a piece.","Sort the cuts and add the stick ends 0 and n. best[i][j] is the cheapest way to make every cut strictly between positions i and j.","best[i][j] = (pos[j] − pos[i]) + min over k between them of best[i][k] + best[k][j]. Fill by increasing width."],"explain":"Cutting in the order 3, 5, 1, 4 costs 7 (the whole stick), then 4 (the piece 3–7), then 3 (piece 0–3), then 2 (piece 3–5): 16 in total, the cheapest order."},
